description: Feature layering rules for src/features
alwaysApply: true
---

# Feature architecture

Every feature under `src/features/<name>/` follows: `repositories/` → `usecases/` → `controllers/` →
`views/`. Nothing skips a layer.

- **Repositories** (`repositories/<name>.repository.ts`): transport only. Wrap `apiJson` calls. No
  business rules, no React, no state.
- **Use cases** (`usecases/<action>-<entity>.usecase.ts`): one exported function per file, one repository
  call, unwrap via `isAppSuccess`/`appErrorMessage`, throw `Error` on failure. Never called from a view.
- **Controllers** (`controllers/<name>.controller.ts`, a `"use client"` hook): own transient UI state,
  orchestrate use cases via React Query `useMutation`/`useQuery`. Never call a repository directly. Export
  a `<Name>ViewModel` type via `ReturnType<typeof use<Name>Controller>`.
- **Views** (`views/<name>.view.tsx`): presentational only. Take the controller's view-model as props,
  render, call `actions.*`. No `apiJson`, `useQuery`, `useMutation`, or repository/usecase imports.
- Route pages under `src/app/` are thin: call the controller hook, render the view with its result.
  Business/data logic does not live in a page component — see `src/features/users/` for the reference
  shape (not the old `src/features/admin/`, which no longer exists after this rule was added).
